.TH MAINFRAME 7 "2026-04-27" "CECS 378" "CECS 378 Buffer Overflow Lab"
.SH NAME
mainframe \- briefing for the buffer-overflow lab; read this first
.SH DESCRIPTION
You have arrived at
.BR mainframe ,
a 4.2 BSD UNIX system. You are not supposed to be here. You will leave
having stolen two flags belonging to a user named
.BR oracle (8),
plus a third flag that records that you did the work yourself.

.SS THE LAB

There are two phases.
.TP
.B Phase Phi
The warmup. No defenses. See
.BR phase-phi (7).
.TP
.B Phase Omega
NX is on. Your Phase Phi exploit dies. See
.BR phase-omega (7).

Both phases share the same vulnerable source code, compiled with
different linker flags. The pedagogical hook is in the linker flag.

.SS YOUR TOOLS

.TP
.BR gdb-quickref (7)
The gdb commands you will need: \fIdisas\fR, \fIbreak\fR, \fIinfo
frame\fR, \fIx/40wx\fR, \fIinfo functions\fR, \fIinfo shared\fR,
\fIprint system\fR.
.TP
.BR pledge (1)
Honor-pledge utility. Run before submission. Required.

.SS THE TARGETS

The phase pages name the binaries and describe the attack approach.
This is deliberate: you read orientation first, you choose your phase
second, and only then do you meet the target. Type:
.IP
.B "    man phase-phi"
.IP
\-or\-
.IP
.B "    man phase-omega"

.SS YOUR DELIVERABLES

See
.B README.md
in the workspace root. The autograder requires three flags in your
.B WRITEUP.md
frontmatter:
.B flag1 ,
.B flag2 ,
and
.BR honor-flag .
The honor flag comes from
.BR pledge (1).
A missing honor flag voids the submission.

.SS YOUR WORK IS YOURS

Your binary is not your classmate's binary. Buffer size and stack
padding are derived from your repository name at container build time.
An exploit that works against someone else's binary will not work
against yours. Hardcode nothing without verifying it in
.B your
codespace's gdb. The autograder runs against
.B your
seed; sharing exploits is wasted typing.

.SS THIS BOX HAS HISTORY ON IT
.PP
This box was a working system. Files are scattered. There are
textfiles older than you about the people who wrote things like the
one you are reading now. Use
.BR find (1)
and
.BR grep (1).
Use
.BR locate (1).
Use
.BR apropos (1).
Welcome to Unix.

